# What putting the 2004 chat housing back over the seated pack changes.
#
#   gate_diff.py --expect tools/porcelain_gate/expect/chat-housing.expect <pairs...>
#
# Baseline: the commit this branch left (83c75b410), captured with the same
# binary family and the same harness. Do NOT concatenate this with
# gameframe-layout.expect or desktop-frame-chatstrip.expect: each of those
# declares what a DIFFERENT change did against a different baseline, and every
# one of their lines would be stale against this one -- which the comparator
# fails on, correctly.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The defect, and why the fix adds a picture rather than moving a box.
#
# Classic Fixed seats the lane's 519x165 chat pack at 17,338, flush with the
# canvas floor. That is right and it is not changed here: 165 rows is what
# buys interface 162 the eight message lines the lane's own frame shows, and
# 338 is the row that leaves no strip of the frame's own filter band standing
# beside the pack's bar.
#
# But 17,338 519x165 is the whole 2004 chat band. `backhmid2` starts at 338,
# `backleft2`'s sheet runs to 17 and `backvmid3` starts at 496 -- so the board
# above the chat hole, the torn edge ripped into it, the torn edge down the
# left and the torn edge down the right all went behind the pack's own flat
# parchment. What the player saw was a hard-edged rectangle: a straight
# horizontal cut across the stone at the top, and a straight seam into the
# frame's columns at either side, on a frame whose every other opening is
# ripped paper.
#
# There is no room to give those rows back. 4 + 334 + 165 = 503 exactly: the
# scene, the pack and nothing else fill the canvas, and any row spent on the
# board is a row off the message pane. So the housing is DRAWN AGAIN over the
# pack, from the frame's own three pieces, with everything that is not rock
# left transparent -- and it is drawn in the eight rows and eleven columns the
# pack's own layout leaves free, so no line, no scrollbar and no glyph is
# covered. The message pane is pixel-for-pixel what it was; only the margin
# around it changed.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. One new owned piece, on the three lanes that run Classic Fixed over an
#    OldSchool toplevel: classic548, classic161, and remount164, which starts
#    on Classic Fixed before it switches away.
#
# It is a piece like the surround's other thirteen, and it is LAST in the blit
# list so that no existing piece is renumbered -- an owned key here is the
# piece's index, and one inserted in the middle would report thirteen controls
# as moved when nothing moved. Its depth is the CHAT rather than the viewport,
# which is the only thing that puts it over a surface every other piece is
# raised under, and it is declared visible_with the chat so it goes away when
# the chatbox does.
owned-add piece.13 = the 2004 board and torn edge, re-cut and drawn back over the pack that covered them; it is last in the blit list so that no other piece is renumbered

# 2. The same picture, said about the capture line.
#
# A plugin-owned node has no component id (0xffffffff) and the box is the
# housing band's own: x=0 because `backleft2` leaves six columns of sheet
# OUTSIDE the pack and they are part of the same defect, 553 because that is
# `backhmid2`'s width -- the band from the canvas edge to the tab column --
# and 338x165 because the housing is exactly the pack's rows.
only-after BOUNDS com=0xffffffff (65535|65535) type=15 graphic=-1 hidden=0 abs=0,338 553x165 = the housing band itself: a new owned picture, at the band's origin and the band's width
